X_TooBib = {publisher: ReWriteParse be.get(s)= be.get(dest)=},
X_TooBib = {author: Anderson , Yasmin Tarek and Priest , Katherine and Zastre , Jason},
abstract = {Alzheimer's Disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by cognitive decline and extensive neuronal loss, largely driven by amyloid beta (Abeta) accumulation and associated cellular stress. Vitamin B1 (thiamine) supplementation has demonstrated cognitive benefits in clinical AD studies, however, the mechanisms underlying thiamine's neuroprotective effects remain unclear. Here, we investigated whether thiamine mitigates Abeta(1-42)-induced neurotoxicity by suppressing hypoxia-inducible factor-1 alpha (HIF-1alpha), a transcriptional stress factor regulating many proapoptotic and progressive amyloidogenic pathways. Exposure of neuronal cells to Abeta(1-42) oligomers increased reactive oxygen species (ROS) accumulation, decreased intracellular Fe(2+), and induced HIF-1alpha stabilization. HIF-1alpha activation by Abeta(1-42) promoted apoptosis through increased endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress and increased mitochondrial dimerization of BNIP3. Thiamine supplementation significantly reduced cellular ROS levels, preserved intracellular Fe(2+) levels, and restored prolyl hydroxylase (PHD) activity to promote HIF-1alpha hydroxylation and degradation. Suppression of HIF-1alpha by thiamine attenuated ER and BNIP3-driven apoptotic pathways and preserved neuronal viability. Thiamine further mitigated HIF-1alpha-mediated amyloidogenic progression, limiting feedback toxicity caused by Abeta(1-42). These results demonstrate that thiamine protects against Abeta(1-42)-mediated neurotoxicity by reducing ROS, preserving Fe(2+), and inhibiting HIF-1alpha-driven pathological cascades. Overall, this study identified a novel mechanism for thiamine's neuroprotective role, further supporting its therapeutic potential to limit neurodegenerative progression in AD.},
